// The boss journal: the Hollow King, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).hollowking = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "The King walks after whoever holds him, like the others did, but his whole hall answers him. Bells, knights, coffins. Too much to watch from one patch of ground. Gave ground.",
    bloodreaver: "The old bones hit like he still has meat on them. A greatsword that leaves the wound open! I have not bled like that in years. I like him already.",
    runeguard: "The Barrow King keeps a court, and a court has procedure: he calls, and a beat later the hall answers. Every sign he gives comes back to be read again. Not foreseen; now it is.",
    gorgemaw: "My host keeps a cold table: no salt, no fire, every dish served twice, once by him and again by his guests. A dead man's kitchen. I will be back for every course.",
    oathbroker: "The Crown holds court in his own tomb and reads out charges against the living. I have never argued before a dead judge. His terms are strict, and he collects in full. So noted.",
    ironpalm: "The King calls, and his hall answers. Bells, knights, coffins: every word said twice. Today I listened too little. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Long hall, bad light. The quarry walks after our knight, and everything it does comes back off the walls a beat later. No line yet.",
    shadowblade: "The mark keeps guards who remember everything, and an eye one must never meet. Charming. A whole court watching, and still nobody watching me.",
    pyromancer: "A cold hall, dead braziers, and a king who glares! He hates being looked at. I hate not being looked at. One of us will have to give way, and it won't be my eyebrows.",
    thermist: "Cold. All of him. The hall, the light, the knights. Then the bells rang and I ran hot right to the end, and the wrong way, twice. Back to the middle, next time.",
    driftblade: "A long dark hall to run through, knights along the walls and coffins in the bays, and a king you must not look at, which means turning on the run, which I can do, mostly.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man refuses to stay dead and has brought his own chorus: knights, bells, coffins. Enormous staging. The company was badly under-rehearsed. (Curtain, early.)",
    cleric: "A poor king who would not lie down, in a hall full of people who never got to. He passes sentences, and the light is the only appeal. Dawn keep us; we will need it.",
    grovekeeper: "The old king is winter come indoors: slow, cold and certain, and everything he does comes round again like a season. My lot were caught by the frost today.",
    chronomancer: "His Majesty does everything twice: once himself, and once through his court, a beat later. I will have heard that echo before. Today, oddly, I had not.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one dead king, cold, very much awake. Ailments observed: more than I could label in one night. The shelves will need restocking. One lantern broke, not deliberately.",
    threadweaver: "His lordship keeps a hall full of loose threads: knights, bells, coffins, lanterns. My little stitches wandered all over it. A sloppier hem I have never seen, and it was mine.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The King went down and stayed down. The line held through the dark to the last lantern. Something in my footing is different tonight. Steadier. Held.",
    bloodreaver: "The big one fell, and every scar he gave me is still warm. Best fight of my life. Something woke in me down there and has not gone back to sleep.",
    runeguard: "The Barrow King is judged at last. I read every omen in his hall, and tonight I find signs in myself I never inscribed. As foreseen? Not quite.",
    gorgemaw: "My host has served his last course and left the table for good. I ate the whole night through. And yet there is a new hunger in me now, one I cannot name.",
    oathbroker: "The Crown's account is closed and the court dismissed, every debt settled. Though I find a clause in myself I do not remember signing. Countersigned, all the same.",
    ironpalm: "The King spoke his last word, and we answered it. Since then, something in me speaks with a voice I have not heard before. I listen. Breathe.",
    ranger: "The old stag is down, and the moon still up. Something sharper in my eye since. Clean line.",
    shadowblade: "The mark fell, and for once I looked it in the face. Something in my shadow moves differently now. It never saw me. I am not sure I see me.",
    pyromancer: "He fell! The moon still up, the hall still dark, and me the brightest thing in it. Something new is smouldering in me, and I adore it. Mostly unsinged.",
    thermist: "Cold all night. Then he fell, and I ran hot with it. Now there is a third thing in me, neither hot nor cold, waiting. Back to the middle. For now.",
    driftblade: "He fell and the hall went quiet and I kept running anyway, out through the doors and round the barrow, and there's something new in the wind, like a road not yet taken.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for our leading man, and the whole company standing at the end. My Doll bowed without being asked, which is new. (Applause. Real applause.)",
    cleric: "The king is at rest, and every one of us walked out into the dawn. Something in the light answers me differently now. Thank the light.",
    grovekeeper: "The long winter is over, and my lot all came through it. Something took root in me down there; I can feel it waiting for spring. Plant first.",
    chronomancer: "We beat him before the moon set, and I did not have to undo a thing. That's new. Something in me already knows what comes next; I will have found out.",
    alembist: "Result: one king, deceased, permanently. Shelves empty, every patient alive. A new reagent has appeared in me that I certainly did not brew. Labelling it later.",
    threadweaver: "His lordship is unpicked for good, and the whole hem came home. There is a new thread in me now, in a colour I don't recognise. Pulled it tight.",
  },
  moves: {
    'Barrow Knell': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The bell tolled and it reached every one of us. Nothing to block. I kept the line together near our healer, footing set, and we took it standing. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The bell rang right through my ribs. Barely a scratch, shameful, but the others feel it, so I kept the big one turned away and let them be mended before it tolled.",
      runeguard: "The omen: he lifts his head, and the bells draw breath. The answer: nobody escapes the toll, so each of us must be whole before it sounds. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The bell is a course served to the whole table at once, and I cannot eat it for anyone. So we sat close, came to it well fed, and swallowed it together. Thin broth.",
      oathbroker: "The Knell is a levy on every one of the undersigned, payable in full; no clause excuses anyone. The fine print: arrive at it already mended. We did. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "The bell spoke to all of us at once. There is no answering it. We came to it whole and let it pass through us. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Bell tolled. Reached everyone; no cover from it. Mended before, kept drawing through it. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The bell found everyone, even me, which was dreadfully rude. Nothing to hide behind, so I simply made sure I was whole when it tolled, and went back to work.",
      pyromancer: "The bell rang so loud my hair stood on end! There's no escaping it, so I made sure I wasn't already half-scorched when it came, and kept casting right through. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "The toll goes through everyone. Cold fact. Be whole before it. Then it hit and I ran hot with it, rattled, still swinging. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The bell rang through the whole hall and there's no running through it or round it, nowhere to go at all, which offends me, so I came to it whole and kept moving through the sound.",
      marionettist: "The great bell tolled through the whole house, stalls and wings alike. No exits for anyone; the company came to it whole and played on. (The Doll did not flinch.)",
      cleric: "The bell tolls for every one of us. I mended everyone before it came, not after, and laid a little grace over the faithful as he raised his head. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The bell comes like hail: no cover, all my lot at once. Plant first. I had the mending growing in everyone before it tolled.",
      chronomancer: "The toll lands a beat after he lifts his head. I had everyone mended in the moment before; next time, which was last time, I'll start a beat earlier.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the bell, felt by everyone, no hiding from it. Remedy: the whole party whole before it tolls, the big brew poured over everyone just before. Keep it on the front shelf.",
      threadweaver: "The bell pulled on every thread at once. Nothing to dodge, so I had the whole hem mended before it tolled and drew everyone in close. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Sceptre Sweep': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sweeps the sceptre across all the ground in front of him, and he faces me. His feet set as he winds it. I stepped round to his flank, the line already behind him. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The sceptre swept the whole front of the room, and I was in front, of course. He can't turn once he winds it, so I stepped round his side and let it whistle past. Not worth the scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the sceptre drawn back, his feet set; once begun, he will not turn. The answer: leave the ground before him entirely, for his side or his back, and return once it has passed.",
      gorgemaw: "The sweep is laid across the whole front of the table, and my host cannot turn the platter once he lifts it. The others dine at his back; some nights I bite into it myself. Generous, and bland.",
      oathbroker: "The terms of the Sweep: all ground before him is liable, and once he draws the sceptre he may not amend his facing. The fine print is how wide it reaches. The undersigned settled behind him.",
      ironpalm: "He drew the sceptre back and his feet set. A long word, for whatever stands before him. I stepped to his side, and the word went past. Then I answered.",
      ranger: "Sweep covers the whole front of it, wide, wall to wall. Its feet lock as it winds. Kept to its back quarter and drew. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark swept the whole front of the hall, which concerned me not at all; I live behind it. The others learned to join me there. It never saw any of us.",
      pyromancer: "He swept the whole front of the hall with that sceptre, and it glowed the colour of trouble right to the far wall! I moved behind him, sulking, and finished the spell from there.",
      thermist: "Wide arc, front only. Feet set; he won't turn. Go behind. I went, quick and hot, and came out the far side still burning.",
      driftblade: "He plants his feet and sweeps the whole front of the hall, so I went round him, through the gap at his side and out the back, and he couldn't turn to follow, never landed.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man sweeps the entire front of the stage and cannot turn once he begins. The company exits upstage, behind him; my Doll was there already. (A rare clean scene.)",
      cleric: "He swept the whole hall before him, and our young archer stood at the wrong end of it. Behind him, dear, always behind; he cannot turn once he begins. Thank the light she lived.",
      grovekeeper: "The sweep comes on like a gale across the front of the field, and nothing standing there sees the season out. Once his feet root he can't turn, so my lot shelter behind him.",
      chronomancer: "He sets his feet, then sweeps the whole front of the hall. Behind him, that beat is quiet. I'll have been standing there already; this time I was.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the sceptre, across the whole front of the room. Note: his feet fix once he winds it. Remedy: stand at his back. Cheap, effective, no brewing required.",
      threadweaver: "He swept the whole front of the hall, and one of my little stitches was snagged right in it. Behind him, everyone; he can't turn once he starts. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Royal Gaze': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "I was trained to face the enemy. He punishes it. When his eyes lit I turned my back on him in the last instant and turned again after. Held, though it went against the grain.",
      bloodreaver: "Looked the big one in the eye once. It froze me where I stood and hurt more than it should, and the second time hurt worse. Now I turn my back when his eyes kindle. Shameful, but sensible.",
      runeguard: "The omen: his eyes kindle beneath the crown. The answer: look elsewhere when it lands, even mid-step; whoever meets his gaze is held fast and marked, and a second meeting is crueller. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host's stare is the one dish I will not touch. Whoever meets his eyes is pinned to their chair, and served worse the second time. So I turned my back on him, rudely. Sent it back.",
      oathbroker: "The Gaze binds whoever meets it, and a second meeting costs more. The fine print: it often lands as a knight swings, so one must dodge facing away from him. The undersigned looked elsewhere. Countersigned.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke with his eyes. Some words should not be answered. I turned away until it passed, then turned back to him. I breathed.",
      ranger: "Its eyes light. Don't look. Turned my back, waited the beat, turned round and drew. Its stare pins you to the spot.",
      shadowblade: "The mark wishes to be looked at. I never look at a mark from the front anyway; I simply turned my back when its eyes lit. Those who stared were nailed to the floor. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He wanted my full attention! I gave him my back instead, which he deserved, and the stare slid right off me. The archer looked, and stood there frozen like a statue.",
      thermist: "Eyes kindle. Turn away. Cold rule. I looked once, hot and curious, and froze stiff where I stood. After that I kept my back to him, and kept still in my head.",
      driftblade: "His stare pins you to the floor, the worst thing there is, so when his eyes lit I ran on facing away from him, round the hall with my back to him the whole way through it.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man demands to be looked at, and whoever obliges is frozen mid-line. The company turns its back on cue. My Doll too; she has no eyes, but I turn her anyway. (Exit, facing away.)",
      cleric: "Our brave knight met the king's eyes and stood frozen, and it hurt him; the second time, more. Turn away when his eyes light, all of you. I watch the wall now, and pray.",
      grovekeeper: "His stare comes on like a killing frost, and whatever faces it stiffens. My lot learned to turn their backs, like sheep turning from the wind, then turn round again after.",
      chronomancer: "His eyes light, and on the next beat whoever is looking at him is held. Looked once, held; looked twice, worse. I turn away on the beat now. Last time, I knew that already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: his stare. Symptoms: frozen where you stand, worse on a second dose. Remedy: your back to him as it lands, even mid-step. Note: it likes to arrive while the knights are swinging.",
      threadweaver: "Whoever looks him in the eye is pinned like cloth under a needle, and it pricks worse the second time. My little stitches turn their backs on him now. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Royal Audience': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "His crown blazed, and this time he wanted to be faced. I always face him; for once that was the whole duty. The line turned to him with me. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "When the crown blazes, look the big one right in the face. Easy! Those who showed him their backs were frozen and hurt. I grinned at him the whole time.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the crown blazes, not the eyes. The answer is the Gaze reversed: face him squarely as it lands. A careless reader confuses the two signs; I very nearly did. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "My host's crown blazed, and he demanded every guest face the head of the table. Turn your back on this host and you're pinned to your chair. I faced him with relish. Well presented, at least.",
      oathbroker: "The Audience reverses the terms of the Gaze: he must be faced as it lands. The fine print is telling the crown from the eyes. Those who turned away were held in contempt. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "His crown blazed. This time he wished to be heard. I faced him and listened. Those who turned away were held. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Crown burns, not eyes: face it. Back to it and you're pinned. Watched the head, turned square. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The crown blazed and the mark demanded to be faced. Showing it my back, my usual habit, earns a freezing. So I looked at it, just this once, from just behind its shoulder.",
      pyromancer: "His crown blazed the colour of trouble, and for once he wanted us looking! Finally, a man of taste. I faced him and kept casting. Those who turned away froze stiff.",
      thermist: "Crown blazes: face him. Eyes kindle: look away. Cold and simple. I mixed them up once, ran hot, and froze solid with my back to him.",
      driftblade: "The crown blazed and he wanted every face on him, so I kept running round him with my eyes on him the whole way, sideways through the hall, which is harder than it sounds.",
      marionettist: "When the crown blazes, our leading man demands the house face the stage. Every member of the company turns to him; backs to the star are frozen. (The Doll faced him beautifully.)",
      cleric: "When his crown blazes he must be faced; when his eyes light, he must not. Our poor archer confused them and stood frozen. I faced him and kept praying. Dawn keep her.",
      grovekeeper: "The crown blazes like a summer sun, and this time my lot turn their faces to it, like flowers. Back to it and you're frozen. Different weather, different answer.",
      chronomancer: "Crown, not eyes: on that beat he must be faced. I will have turned to him a moment early, I'm sure. I did. Those with their backs to him were held.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the blazing crown. Symptoms: freezing, but only for those with their backs to him. Remedy: face him as it lands. Shelve it well apart from the Gaze; the labels look alike.",
      threadweaver: "His crown blazed, and every stitch had to face him. One turned away and was pinned fast. A sloppy seam, and I told her so. Faced him, and pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Grave Cleave': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The greatsword came down on me alone, and it leaves the wound open after. I raised the shield in the last instant, kept the line clear of the ground in front of him, and bled quietly. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The greatsword! A deep one, and it keeps bleeding long after. Lovely. I braced, kept him turned away from the rest, let it land, then drank it back while it was still running.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the greatsword lifted over me alone. The answer: a ward laid a breath before, the others well clear of the ground before him, and our healers ready for the bleeding that follows. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The greatsword is served to me alone, a heavy cut with a sauce that keeps running long after. I opened wide just before it, kept the others off my plate, and chewed. Rare, and good.",
      oathbroker: "The Cleave is payable by whoever stands before him, and the fine print is the bleeding that follows, compounding. I accepted delivery behind every protection I had, the undersigned well clear. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "The greatsword came for me. I met it at its last instant with everything raised. The wound kept speaking after; I let it, and answered him. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Big blade, meant for our knight. Nobody else in front of it. Watched from the flank; the wound kept bleeding after. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The greatsword came down on our knight, as it should. The front of the mark is no place for anyone else. I stayed at its back and let our knight deal with its manners.",
      pyromancer: "The greatsword came down on our knight with a terrible gleam! None of my business, provided I'm nowhere in front of him. I kept well clear. Hair intact.",
      thermist: "Greatsword, for our knight. Stay clear of his front. Then I watched it land and ran hot, because the wound kept bleeding long after.",
      driftblade: "The greatsword is for our knight alone, so I kept out of the ground in front of him and went round and through behind instead, and watched the wound keep running after.",
      marionettist: "The leading man's great dramatic stroke is for our knight alone; the rest of the company keeps off the front of the stage. My Doll stays at his back. (The wound bled on through the next scene.)",
      cleric: "The greatsword falls on our knight, and the wound stays open after. I lay the light on him before it lands, and keep mending until the bleeding stops. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "The greatsword falls on our knight like a felled oak, and the wound weeps after like sap. I plant the mending on him before the blow, and tend it while he bleeds.",
      chronomancer: "The greatsword lands on our knight, and the bleeding keeps time after it, beat by beat. I had him shielded before, and mended through the echo. I'll have done this before.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the greatsword on our knight, then a bleeding that keeps on. Remedy: a protective dose before it falls, a slow cure for the bleeding after. Keep both on the near shelf.",
      threadweaver: "The greatsword landed on our knight, and I ran a thread to take some of it off him. The wound keeps pulling after; I kept the seam mended till it closed.",
    } },
    'Barrow Stomp': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He stamped, and the ground round his feet broke. I gave ground, out past the edge with the line, and came back to him after. Gave ground. Then held.",
      bloodreaver: "He stamps, and the ground round him goes up. Tempting! But it's a dull hurt with nothing to it, so I stepped out with the others and came straight back to his face.",
      runeguard: "The omen: his foot raised, a ring of earth outlined about him. The answer: everyone, myself included, beyond its edge before it falls; then back in. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A round platter of broken ground, served about my host's feet. I pushed back from the table with the others and came back after; some nights I take a bite. Crumbly.",
      oathbroker: "The Stomp's terms: anyone within the ring at his feet is liable. Simple. The fine print is that his knights remember it, and present it again later. The undersigned stepped clear. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He stamped. A short word, said close. I stepped back out of its reach, then in again to answer. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Ring at its feet. I'm always outside it. The close ones had to step out. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark stamped, and the floor all round it broke. Even its back is no shelter from that. I stepped out past the edge and slipped back in after. It never saw me leave.",
      pyromancer: "The floor round his feet glowed, and I was well out of it, for once, at a sensible distance! The ones close to him had to step out smartly. Entirely unsinged.",
      thermist: "Ring at his feet. Step out. Cold, done. Then back in, hot, swinging before the dust had settled.",
      driftblade: "He stamps and the ground round him breaks, so I went out through the edge before it fell and came straight back in through the dust after, never landed.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man stamps, and the floor about his feet breaks. The company steps back to the wings; my Doll takes one dainty step out and returns on cue. (Light applause.)",
      cleric: "He stamps, and the ground about him breaks. Those close to him must step back out of it. Our brave knight stayed in once; it was the last time. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The ground round him heaves like frost in early spring. My lot step back past the edge, then return. No planting needed if nobody's standing in it.",
      chronomancer: "He lifts his foot; on the next beat the ground about him breaks. Out on the lift, back in on the after-beat. I'll have known that for ages.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the ground at his feet, broken. Remedy: out of the ring before it falls. No reagents required. Note: his knights serve it again later from where they stand.",
      threadweaver: "He stamped, and the stitches near him had to step back out of the ring. A neat little hem round him, and nobody inside it. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Royal Doom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sentenced one of ours, never me. Lanterns appeared on the floor, and the doomed cannot lift one. I picked one up and carried it to her side. The light cured it. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "He dooms one of the others, never me, which is insulting. The doomed can't lift a lantern, so I grabbed one off the floor and ran it to them. Light cures it. Not a scratch to show for it.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a sigil of death over one of us, never over me. The answer: lanterns appear on the floor; another must lift one and bring its light near, for the doomed cannot. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host passes sentence on one of my guests. Lanterns appear about the hall; the doomed may not touch them, so I picked one up and brought it to the table. Light is the only sauce for it.",
      oathbroker: "Doom is a sentence on one of the undersigned, with a short term. The fine print: the doomed may not take up a lantern; a co-signatory must carry one to them. I did. Settled.",
      ironpalm: "He named one of us for death. The doomed cannot lift a lantern. I carried one to her. The light answered him.",
      ranger: "Sigil over one of ours. Lanterns down round the hall; the marked one can't lift one. Nearest hand fetches it. I ran it over.",
      shadowblade: "The mark sentenced a friend. The doomed cannot lift a lantern, so I slipped across the hall, took one off the floor and delivered it like a secret. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He doomed me! Me! I couldn't even lift a lantern, so I stood where everyone could reach me and waited, glowing with indignation, until someone brought the light.",
      thermist: "Marked for death. Chilled right through. The doomed can't lift a lantern, so I stood still where I could be found. Our knight brought one, and the cold went out of me at once.",
      driftblade: "He doomed our archer, who can't lift a lantern while doomed, so I went through the hall to the nearest one, scooped it up on the run, and through to her before it came due.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man passes sentence on a member of the company. The doomed may not touch the lanterns, so another must carry one on. (Enter the knight, stage right, with the light. Well done.)",
      cleric: "He passes sentence on one of us, and only the light answers it. The doomed cannot carry it themselves; someone else brings a lantern to them. I brought it this time. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "His sentence lands on one of my lot like blight. Lanterns appear on the ground; the blighted can't carry one, so someone hale fetches it over. Light cures it the way sun cures frost.",
      chronomancer: "Sentence passed, on a short clock. The doomed cannot lift a lantern, so someone else must carry one over before the last beat. I did, with a beat to spare.",
      alembist: "Ailment: Doom. Fatal if neglected. Remedy: lantern light, carried over by someone not doomed; the patient cannot dose herself. Note: spare lanterns gutter out once the cure is done.",
      threadweaver: "He doomed one of my little stitches, and she can't fetch a lantern herself. Another stitch carries the light to her; a lantern left on the floor helps nobody. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Crown of Graves': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The whole hall broke open, all but the ground right at his feet. I am always there. The line came in close behind me. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The whole hall erupted except right up against the big one, which is exactly where I live. The others came in and hugged him with me. Barely a scratch, for once.",
      runeguard: "The omen: graves open across the hall, but not at his feet. The answer: close in on him, inside the quiet middle, before it lands. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The whole hall was served, except the little plate at my host's feet. I was already sitting there, and the others crowded in beside me. Too much table, not enough food.",
      oathbroker: "The Crown of Graves claims every inch of the hall but the ground beside him. The fine print: when a knight repeats it, the quiet is beside the knight. The undersigned closed in. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke to the whole hall, but not to those beside him. I was beside him. The others came close. We listened in the quiet. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hate this one. Whole hall erupts but the ground at its feet. Gave up the distance, came right in close. Short line.",
      shadowblade: "The whole hall broke open, all but the few paces round the mark. Close is where I live. The others had to come and crowd my lovely spot. It never saw any of us.",
      pyromancer: "The whole hall lit up except the little ring at his feet! I had to give up my lovely distant spot and run right up to him. Hated every step. Survived beautifully.",
      thermist: "Everywhere but close. Cold read: in, now. Then I ran hot all the way to his feet, and the hall burst behind me.",
      driftblade: "The whole hall breaks open except right up against him, so I cut through the middle and in to his feet and circled there, close enough to touch, until it was done.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man claims the entire stage except his own spotlight. The company rushes in to share it; my Doll too, at his very knee. (Crowded, but nobody missed the cue.)",
      cleric: "The whole hall breaks open except the quiet ground at his feet. I gathered everyone in close to him, near enough to touch, and nobody fell. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The whole field heaves except the patch at his feet, like a frost that spares the hearth. My lot crowd in close to him. Snug, if cold.",
      chronomancer: "On the downbeat the whole hall breaks open but the ground at his feet. In on the beat before, all of us. I will have run in early; I did.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the whole hall, cracked open. Remedy: stand at his feet, where it doesn't reach. Note for the shelf: from a knight, the safe ground is at that knight's feet instead.",
      threadweaver: "Everywhere broke but the ground at his feet, so I pulled the whole hem in tight round him. Not one loose stitch left out in the hall.",
    } },
    'Echo Knights': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two dead knights rose by the walls. Each remembers one of his swings as he makes it, then swings it again from where it stands. I watch what each one learned, and move the line to match. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "His old guard rose and copied his swings, later, from where they stood. Two more friends to bleed for! I watch which swing each one caught, and keep out of it anyway.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a swing flashes on a knight as he makes it; that knight now holds it. The answer: read each knight's sigil, and when he replays it, dodge it as if the King swung it from there. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "His guard reheat his dishes: each knight keeps one of his swings and serves it again from its own seat by the wall. Leftovers, but still hot. I note what each carried off, and dine accordingly.",
      oathbroker: "The knights are witnesses: each remembers one swing as it is made and testifies to it later, from where it stands, aimed at the middle. The fine print: their memory is exact. The undersigned avoided the testimony.",
      ironpalm: "His knights repeat his words. Each keeps one, and says it again from where it stands. I listen to what each one heard. Then I step aside.",
      ranger: "Two knights by the walls. Each keeps a swing it saw, replays it from there toward the middle. Watch which one it took. New angle, same shape.",
      shadowblade: "Two knights rise, and each memorises one of the mark's swings, then repeats it from its own spot by the wall. Copycats. I note which trick each has stolen, and stand where it cannot reach.",
      pyromancer: "Two knights rose and copied his swings! The nerve. Each repeats one, later, from its own spot. I watch which one each stole and move my lovely spot to match.",
      thermist: "Each knight takes one swing as he makes it. Keeps it cold. Replays it from its wall. Read the knight, not him. Moved, hot, and the floor burst behind me.",
      driftblade: "His knights each catch one of his swings and throw it again from the walls, so I watch which swing flashed on which knight and run my path through whatever gaps they leave.",
      marionettist: "Two understudies by the walls, each learning one of the star's lines as he delivers it and performing it again later from their own marks. The company watches who learned what. (Prompt corner, attentive.)",
      cleric: "Two of his old guard rose, each keeping one of his swings to use again from where they stand. I watch which one each knight took, and lead my friends clear of it.",
      grovekeeper: "The knights keep his swings the way soil keeps seed, and each grows one again later from where it stands. My lot watch what each one took, and stand clear of the crop.",
      chronomancer: "The knights are his echo: each catches one swing on the beat he makes it and plays it back later from the walls. I've always liked an echo. I'll have dodged both.",
      alembist: "Ailment: two knights, each storing one of his swings and replaying it later from its own spot. Remedy: note which swing went to which knight, and dodge it from that knight's position.",
      threadweaver: "Two knights rose, each holding onto one of his swings to replay from the walls. I keep a thread on which one took what, and move my little stitches out of its way.",
    } },
    'The Charge Sheet': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Banners above his throne lit one at a time, each with one of his swings. Nothing fell. I said the order to the line until we had it. It comes back later. Held. On the hard nights the order is drawn fresh each pull; read the banners every time.",
      bloodreaver: "Banners light up over his throne, one swing on each, and nothing hits! Boring. But the big one swings them all later, in that order, so I learned the order. Annoying. On the hard nights the order changes each pull, so I learned to read, not recite.",
      runeguard: "The omen of omens: banners over the throne, lit in turn, each bearing a swing's sigil, and nothing lands. The answer: inscribe the order now, for Judgement returns it exactly. As foreseen. On the hard nights the order is drawn anew each pull. Inscribe it fresh.",
      gorgemaw: "My host reads out the menu: banners above his throne, a course on each, in order. Nothing served yet. I memorised every dish; a good guest always knows what's coming. On the hard nights the menu changes each time; read it again.",
      oathbroker: "He reads the charges: the banners light in turn, each a swing, and nothing is collected yet. The fine print is the order, which Judgement enforces exactly. Entered in the ledger. On the hard nights the order is redrawn each hearing. Entered anew.",
      ironpalm: "He named his swings in order, on banners. Nothing more. I repeated them to myself until they were mine. Later, he says it all again. On the hard nights it changes each time. I listened again.",
      ranger: "Banners light over the throne. A sign on each, in turn. Remember them. They come back. Hard nights: order changes every pull. Read it fresh.",
      shadowblade: "The mark announced its plans on banners, in order, like an amateur. I committed every one to memory. Judgement is far less rude when you know the running order. On the hard nights the order is different every pull. Read it fresh.",
      pyromancer: "Banners over his throne lit one after another, each with one of his swings! Nothing happened, so I nearly ignored it. Later I very much wished I hadn't. Remember the order! On the hard nights the order changes each time, so I watched properly.",
      thermist: "Banners, lit in turn. Learn the order cold. When it comes back the swings come fast and hot, and there's no time to read them. Hard: order changes every pull. Learn it fresh, cold.",
      driftblade: "The banners light up one after the other over his throne and nothing happens, which bores me, but I learned the order like a route, so later I knew which way to run. On the hard nights the route changes each pull, so I watch the banners.",
      marionettist: "The star reads out his programme: banners above the throne, each act in order, nothing yet performed. The company learns the running order now, or misses every cue later. On the hard nights the programme is rewritten each night. Read it again.",
      cleric: "The banners above his throne light one after another, each marked with one of his swings, and nothing falls. I whispered the order to my friends like a prayer, and we kept it. On the hard nights the order is drawn fresh each pull, so we read it together.",
      grovekeeper: "The banners light in turn like the seasons, a swing on each. Nothing falls yet. Learn the order of the seasons, and you know what's coming. On the hard nights the seasons come in a different order each year. Read them again.",
      chronomancer: "He plays the melody once on the banners, a sigil for each note. Learn it now; later he plays it back fast. I knew it before he finished, which, admittedly, was last time. On the hard nights the melody changes each time. I'll have listened properly.",
      alembist: "Note for the shelf: banners over the throne, lit in order, one swing on each. Nothing lands yet. Remedy for later: the order, written down in my head. Nothing to brew. Note: on the hard nights the order is redrawn each pull. Re-write it.",
      threadweaver: "Banners lit above the throne one after another, a swing on each. Nothing fell. I stitched the order into my memory, because later it all comes back in sequence. On the hard nights the order changes each pull. I re-stitched it each time.",
    } },
    'Coffin Lids': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He tore the lids off coffins on both walls and sent them skidding across the floor. Out of their path. Where they stop, they're cover for what comes next. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Coffin lids skidding at me across the floor! I stepped out of their lanes, reluctantly. They stop out in the hall, and you'll want to hide behind them very soon.",
      runeguard: "The omen: lids rattle on either wall, their paths drawn across the floor. The answer: clear the paths; once they stop, they stand as cover for the Decree that follows. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "He slid two heavy platters down the table from the walls. Not food, alas: coffin lids. Out of their way, then behind them, because the next course is the Decree.",
      oathbroker: "The lids come skidding from the walls along marked lines, and anyone in the path is liable. The fine print: where they come to rest is your shelter for the Decree. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He sent coffin lids along the floor. I stepped out of their path. They stopped. I stood behind one. Then he spoke again.",
      ranger: "Lids off the coffins, skidding in from both walls. Out of their lanes. Where they stop is cover. Mark it.",
      shadowblade: "Coffin lids came skidding across the hall from both walls. One steps out of their way, and then, naturally, behind one. Hiding is my whole vocation.",
      pyromancer: "Coffin lids came skidding at me from the wall! Out of the lane, then behind the lid. I have never been so glad to hide behind a coffin. Don't tell anyone.",
      thermist: "Lids from the walls, along straight lanes. Step aside. Then, where they stop, get behind one. Cold logic, for the hot wave next.",
      driftblade: "Coffin lids came skidding along the floor from both walls and I went across their lanes before they arrived, and through, and doubled back behind one once it stopped.",
      marionettist: "Scenery arrives! Coffin lids slide on from both wings along their marks. The company steps out of their way, then behind them for the next scene. (My Doll takes cover with great dignity.)",
      cleric: "The king tore the lids off his coffins and sent them skidding across the floor. Everyone out of their way, then behind one; those lids are shelter. Thank the light for small mercies.",
      grovekeeper: "Coffin lids slide across the hall like logs on a flood. My lot step out of their path, then shelter in their lee when they settle. The next storm is close behind.",
      chronomancer: "The lids skid in from the walls; a beat later, he decrees. Out of their path on the first beat, behind them on the second. I'll have been hiding there already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: coffin lids, skidding in from the walls along fixed lanes. Remedy: out of the lanes. Note: they stop and stand as cover, which is needed straight after.",
      threadweaver: "Lids came skidding in from both walls. My little stitches stepped out of their way, then tucked in behind them. Neat as a hem.",
    } },
    'Royal Decree': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A wave rolled down the hall from the throne. Only something solid between me and the throne kept it off. I put a coffin lid there and kept my footing behind it. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "A wave of grave-light down the whole hall. Can't take it head-on, not even me. Hid behind a coffin lid with the throne on its far side. Felt cowardly. Felt alive.",
      runeguard: "The omen: light gathering at the throne. The answer: something solid between oneself and the throne, a lid or fallen stone, and nothing less. The lid breaks after. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The Decree rolls down the hall from the throne, and there's no eating it. I crouched behind a coffin lid, throne on the far side. Some dishes are best left to pass.",
      oathbroker: "The Decree is binding on all who are not sheltered. The fine print: the shelter must stand between you and the throne, not merely nearby. A coffin lid sufficed, and broke. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He decreed, and his voice rolled down the whole hall. I put a coffin lid between us and let it pass. The lid did not survive the sentence.",
      ranger: "Wave from the throne, down the whole hall. Needs something solid between you and it. Lid between me and the throne. No line needed.",
      shadowblade: "A wave of grave-light from the throne. One hides behind something solid, with the throne on its far side. I was born for this. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A great wave of grave-light came rolling down from the throne, so bright! I tucked in behind a coffin lid with the throne on its far side. Even my sleeves survived.",
      thermist: "Wave from the throne. Cold. Need something solid between me and it. Lid. Crouched. Then the lid shattered and I came out hot.",
      driftblade: "A wave rolls down from the throne and there's no running through it, so I had to stop, which I hate, and crouch behind a coffin lid with the throne on its far side.",
      marionettist: "The leading man's great decree sweeps the whole house from the throne. The company hides behind the scenery, lids between them and him. (The scenery did not survive. The company did.)",
      cleric: "His decree rolled down from the throne like a cold tide. Only those with something solid between them and the throne were spared, so I gathered everyone behind the lids. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The decree rolls from the throne like flood water. My lot sheltered behind the coffin lids, the throne on the far side, like lambs behind a wall.",
      chronomancer: "The decree comes a beat after the lids settle. Behind them, throne on the far side, by the downbeat. Next time, which was this time, I'll have been there first.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the Decree, from the throne, down the whole hall. Remedy: something solid between you and the throne, lid or fallen stone. The lid breaks; a single-use container.",
      threadweaver: "The decree rolled down from the throne, and only those with a lid between them and it were spared. I tucked my little stitches behind them in a neat row.",
    } },
    'Funeral March': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "His dead marched across the hall in ranks, wall to wall. One lane through them stays open the whole way. I found it and brought the line into it. Held. The open lane is somewhere new each time.",
      bloodreaver: "His dead came marching in ranks from wall to wall! Lovely, but not even I stand in front of that. There's always one open gap through them; I stood in it, sulking. The lane falls somewhere different each time, and nothing marks it.",
      runeguard: "The omen: ranks of the dead forming along one wall. The answer: one lane through them stays open from wall to wall; stand in it, or step in behind a rank once it has passed. On these nights it falls somewhere different each time, and is never painted; read it off the ranks.",
      gorgemaw: "His dead paraded across the table in ranks, a procession of bones. Far too dry to eat. There's always one gap in the procession; I sat in it. It opens somewhere different each time, unpainted.",
      oathbroker: "The March crosses the whole hall, rank upon rank. The fine print: one lane stays open the whole way, and the ground behind a passing rank is safe. The undersigned found the lane. The lane is drawn anew each time, and nothing marks it.",
      ironpalm: "His dead marched across the hall. One silence ran through them, the same the whole way. I stood in it. Breathe. It sits somewhere new each time.",
      ranger: "Ranks of dead crossing the hall, wall to wall. One lane open all the way through. Found it, stood in it. Clean line. Lane's different every time, unmarked.",
      shadowblade: "Ranks of the mark's dead marched through, wall to wall. There is always one gap, and a gap is only a shadow with manners. I stood in it. The lane is somewhere new each time, and nothing marks it.",
      pyromancer: "Rank after rank of his dead came marching through! I found the one gap they all left open and stood there, casting, while the parade went by. Mostly unsinged. It was in a different place each time, unpainted.",
      thermist: "Ranks, wall to wall. Find the gap; it's the same for every rank. Stand in it. Cold. Then the last rank passed, and I ran hot after it. The lane moves each time and nothing shows where; read the ranks.",
      driftblade: "His dead marched through in ranks, wall to wall, and there's always one lane through them, or the gap behind a rank as it passes, so I went through behind one and kept going. It was somewhere new every time, so I read the gap.",
      marionettist: "A procession! Ranks of extras marching from one wing to the other. One aisle stays open all the way across, and the company stands in it. (A spectacular scene.) The aisle is placed anew each night, and no one marks it.",
      cleric: "His dead marched across the hall, rank after rank. One lane through them stayed open, and I called everyone into it. Thank the light, all our faithful stood there in time. It falls somewhere different each time, unmarked.",
      grovekeeper: "His dead came across in rows like a harvest. One furrow stays open all the way through; my lot stood in it and let the rows pass by. It falls somewhere new each season, and nothing paints it.",
      chronomancer: "Rank after rank, a beat apart, and one lane open on every beat. Stand in it and the march passes round you. I'll have known where it was. It lands somewhere new each time, unmarked.",
      alembist: "Ailment: ranks of the dead across the whole hall. Remedy: the one lane they leave open, or the floor just behind a rank once it passes. No brew. Note: the lane is somewhere new each time, and never marked.",
      threadweaver: "His dead marched across the hall in rows. One gap stays open all the way through, like the gap in a seam. My little stitches went into it, one by one. It opens somewhere new each time, unmarked.",
    } },
    'Barrow Bells': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two bells. Each wants a pair standing under it, or the whole line is doomed. I took a bell with a partner and held my ground beneath it. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Two bells, two of us under each. Miss one and everyone gets doomed! I grabbed a partner and a bell and stood there. Not much fun, but nobody died.",
      runeguard: "The omen: two bell-rings drawn on the floor. The answer: a pair beneath each, decided before they're needed, for an unrung bell dooms us all. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Two bells, each laid for two diners. Leave a bell unrung and every guest is doomed. I sat at one with a friend. A shared plate, sadly.",
      oathbroker: "The Bells require two signatories each; an unrung bell defaults on the whole party, every one of us doomed. The fine print is the pair. I countersigned at one bell.",
      ironpalm: "Two bells, two voices to each. Leave one silent and all of us are doomed. I stood beneath one with a friend. We answered together.",
      ranger: "Two bells. A pair under each. Miss one, everyone's doomed. Took the far bell with the healer.",
      shadowblade: "Two bells, each wanting a pair beneath it. I prefer to be alone, but an unrung bell dooms us all, so I stood under one with a friend. Mostly unseen, still.",
      pyromancer: "Two bells, and each wants two of us under it! Miss one and everyone is doomed. I stood under mine with our knight, ringing beautifully.",
      thermist: "Two bells, two people each. Cold arithmetic. Leave one empty, everyone doomed. I ran hot to mine and stood still beneath it with a friend.",
      driftblade: "Two bells, two of us under each, and I had to stand still under mine with a friend, which I hate, because an empty bell dooms every one of us.",
      marionettist: "Two bells, each requiring a duet beneath it. Leave one silent and the whole company is doomed. Pairs, my dears, cast in advance. (My Doll does not count.)",
      cleric: "Two bells, each needing two of us beneath it; if one goes unrung, every one of us is doomed. I paired everyone off before the bells came. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Two bells, two of my lot under each, like planting in pairs. Leave one bare and the whole field is doomed. We paired up early.",
      chronomancer: "Two bells, two to each. Leave one empty and all of us are doomed. The pairs were set a beat before. I'll have chosen mine already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: two bells. Remedy: a pair under each. Note: an empty bell dooms everyone. Settle the pairs before the bells appear.",
      threadweaver: "Two bells, two stitches threaded under each. Leave one empty and the whole hem is doomed. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Lights Out': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Every brazier went dark, then the pair nearest the King flared back up. Whoever stood outside their light was caught. I got the line into the light. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Darkness! Then the braziers nearest the big one flared back, and anyone outside their light got caught by his stare. I stood in the light, scowling.",
      runeguard: "The omen: every brazier dies. The answer: the pair nearest him flare back a breath later; stand within their light before it lands, for his dread eye finds whoever stands in the dark.",
      gorgemaw: "My host blew out the candles. Then the pair nearest him relit, and anyone outside their glow was frozen. I sat by the fire. Atmosphere is everything at dinner.",
      oathbroker: "All lights fail; the braziers nearest him return. The fine print: you must be inside their light when it lands. Those left in the dark were held. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The lights died. Two came back, near him. I stood in their light. In the dark, he speaks to you alone.",
      ranger: "All fires out. The two near it come back. Get in their light. The dark is where it catches you.",
      shadowblade: "Every light went out, and I was delighted, until it became clear this mark punishes the dark. The braziers nearest it relit, and I stood in the light like everyone else. Humiliating.",
      pyromancer: "Every fire went out! An outrage! Then the braziers nearest him flared back, and I stood right in their light, glowing smugly. Out in the dark, you're caught.",
      thermist: "Fires out. Cold. The pair nearest him come back. Get to them, fast, hot. Stand in the light. Back in the warm.",
      driftblade: "Every brazier died and then the two nearest him came back, so I went through the dark straight to the light and circled inside its edge until he'd looked and found nobody.",
      marionettist: "Blackout! Then two spotlights, near our leading man. The company must stand in them before his eye falls on the dark. (Places, everyone. Into the light.)",
      cleric: "Every fire in the hall went out, and then the two nearest him came back. I called my friends into their light. Those left in the dark were caught. Dawn keep us, always.",
      grovekeeper: "The braziers all went out like the end of a season, then the two nearest him rekindled. My lot crowded into their warmth. Those left in the dark were caught.",
      chronomancer: "Every fire out; a beat later two come back, near him. Into their light before the next beat. I'll have been standing there when they lit.",
      alembist: "Ailment: total darkness. Remedy: the braziers nearest him relight; stand in their light. Note: in the dark, he catches you.",
      threadweaver: "The fires all went out, then the two nearest him came back. I drew my little stitches into their light before it landed. No loose threads left in the dark.",
    } },
    'Court of Bones': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sat back on his throne, out of reach, and the coffins down the walls woke one by one. We broke each before it opened. When one did, I took the bones that climbed out. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one sat down, and coffins woke along the walls. Break them before they open! One opened and a skeleton crawled out. I let it bite me first, then broke it.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a coffin glows as it nears opening, in a fixed order down the walls. The answer: break each before it opens; the order never changes. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host retired from the table and left coffins to serve. Each glows before it opens; break it first, or a skeleton climbs out. All bone, no meat. I caught one anyway.",
      oathbroker: "A recess: he retired to his throne while the coffins woke, each in turn. The fine print: break each before it opens, or it releases a skeleton. The undersigned settled most of them.",
      ironpalm: "He sat. The coffins spoke, one by one. We broke each before it opened. One opened. I answered it.",
      ranger: "It sat down, out of reach. Coffins along the walls glow, then open. Break each first. Shot them in order.",
      shadowblade: "The mark retired to its throne, and the coffins along the walls woke one by one. Break each before it opens. Coffins have no eyes; my favourite kind of mark.",
      pyromancer: "He sat down to watch while coffins woke along the walls! Break each one before it opens. I set them alight in order. Delightful.",
      thermist: "Coffins, one by one, glowing. Cold order. Break each before it opens. Then a skeleton crawled out of one I missed, and I ran hot.",
      driftblade: "He sat on his throne and the coffins woke down the walls one after another, so I ran along the bays breaking each one before it could open, the best part of the whole fight.",
      marionettist: "Intermission! Our leading man retires to his throne while coffins awaken down the walls. The company breaks each before it opens. (My Doll smashed several. Bravo.)",
      cleric: "He sat on his throne and woke the coffins down the walls, one by one. My friends broke them before they opened. One skeleton got out, and I kept our knight on his feet.",
      grovekeeper: "Coffins woke along the walls like seeds in spring, one at a time. My lot broke each before it could sprout. Weeding, really.",
      chronomancer: "The coffins wake one at a time, a beat apart, in the same order. Break each before its beat. I'll have known the order. I did.",
      alembist: "Ailment: coffins, waking one by one. Remedy: break each before it opens. Note: an opened coffin spoils into a skeleton. Clear the shelf in order.",
      threadweaver: "He sat on his throne while the coffins woke, one by one. My little stitches broke them before they opened. Tidy work.",
    } },
    'Sceptre Throw': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "From his throne he hurled his sceptre at the one standing farthest from him, and the line followed them until the last moment. Whoever is farthest takes it. I made sure it was someone who could walk clear, and on the harder nights the two farthest both take one. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one threw his sceptre from the throne at whoever stood farthest from him! The line tracks you till a breath before. Walk off the line, don't stand there admiring it. Harder nights, the two farthest.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a line from the throne finds the farthest of us and follows them. The answer: whoever stands farthest leads it away and steps off before it lands; on harder nights, the two farthest. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host threw his sceptre across the room at the guest sitting farthest away! It follows them till the last moment. Keep moving, step off. On harder nights he throws at the two farthest.",
      oathbroker: "The Sceptre Throw is directed at the undersigned farthest from the throne, and tracks them until a moment before it lands. Terms: step aside in time. On the harder terms, the two farthest are named. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He threw his sceptre from the throne at the farthest of us. The line followed. I stepped off it. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Throne to farthest target. Line tracks till just before. Stepped off. Hard: two farthest.",
      shadowblade: "The mark hurled its sceptre from its throne at whoever stood farthest from it. Not me, naturally; I stay close. The line follows until the last moment. Step off it.",
      pyromancer: "He flung his sceptre from the throne at whoever was farthest away! That line follows you around! I stepped off right at the end, like a very graceful cat. Hard nights, the two farthest.",
      thermist: "Cold: sceptre, throne to the farthest of us, tracking until the last moment. Step off. Hot: I was the farthest, and I ran sideways. Two farthest on hard.",
      driftblade: "He threw his sceptre from the throne at the farthest of us and the line chased me, so I ran across it, not along it, and it landed behind me. On harder nights it goes for the two farthest.",
      marionettist: "The king hurls his sceptre from the throne at the player farthest from him. The line follows the performer until the last beat; step off the mark. (Two players on a hard night.)",
      cleric: "He hurled his sceptre from the throne at whoever was farthest from him. The line followed them till just before it landed; I called them to step sideways. Two of us on harder nights.",
      grovekeeper: "A sceptre flew from the throne at the farthest of my lot, the line following them like a vine till the last moment. They stepped off. On harder nights it flies at the two farthest.",
      chronomancer: "The sceptre flies from the throne to whoever is farthest, the line tracking until a beat before. Step off. I'll have been somewhere else. On harder nights, the two farthest.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a sceptre thrown from the throne at the farthest of us, tracking until a moment before. Remedy: step off the line in time. Note: two farthest on harder nights. Whoever is farthest, beware.",
      threadweaver: "He threw his sceptre from the throne at my farthest stitch, the line following them until the end. They stepped off it. On harder nights it goes for the two farthest. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Grave Summons': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The dead gathered on one of the young ones, never me, and the rest of us had to close on them. All but one; on harder nights, a spread follows 3.5 s after, with each of us marked. Close in, then out. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The dead gathered on one of the others! Everyone pile in, nobody left out. And on the harder nights, straight after, a mark over each head. In, then out. I like it when the room runs.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the dead gather on one of the party, never the knight. The answer: all but one gather on them; on harder nights, the marks follow 3.5 s after, so scatter. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The dead sat down at the table beside one guest, and the rest of us were asked to join. All but one, please. On hard nights, small plates follow 3.5 s later: scatter. Crowded, then lonely.",
      oathbroker: "The dead assemble upon one of the undersigned, never the shield. Terms: all but one attend. On the harder terms, a separate mark follows each of us 3.5 s later. Attend, then disperse. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The dead gathered on one of us. We went to them. On the harder nights, marks came after. We scattered. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Dead gathered on one of the others, not the tank. All but one in. Hard nights: marks follow 3.5 s after. In, then out.",
      shadowblade: "The dead gathered on someone else, which is rude of them. I went in, then out. On the harder nights, marks over each of us come 3.5 s later. Keep moving.",
      pyromancer: "The dead gathered on one of us and everyone had to pile in! Then, on hard nights, a mark on every head three and a half seconds later: scatter! Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: dead gather on one of the party; all but one in. Hard: marks over every head 3.5 s later. In, out. Then back to the middle, hot.",
      driftblade: "The dead gathered on one of us and I had to run in and stand close, which I hate. Then on hard nights a mark over each head 3.5 s later and I ran straight out.",
      marionettist: "The dead gather on one player, never the lead. The company closes on them, all but one; on a hard night, the marks follow 3.5 s behind. (Close ranks, then fan out.)",
      cleric: "The dead gathered on one of my friends, never our knight. We closed on them, all but one, and on harder nights the marks came 3.5 s later, so we scattered at once. I mended everyone after.",
      grovekeeper: "The dead gathered on one of my lot like moss on a stone. We drew in close. On harder nights the marks follow 3.5 s after, and the moss must be scattered. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "The dead gather on one of us, never the knight. Everyone in, all but one. On harder nights the marks follow 3.5 s later: in, then out. I'll have moved already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the dead gather on one of us. Remedy: all but one close on them; on harder nights, a mark on each of us 3.5 s later, so out again at once. Note: crowd, then disperse.",
      threadweaver: "The dead gathered on one of my little stitches. I drew the rest in, all but one; on harder nights the marks follow 3.5 s later, and the threads must go slack again at once. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Grave Marks': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A mark over every one of us, each wanting its own ground. The line opened up, each to their own patch, then closed again. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "A mark over every head, mine too. Mine would hurt the others, so I took my own patch of floor. Two marks on one person is the one that hurts.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a circle at everyone's feet. The answer: each of us on our own ground, no circle touching another. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A small plate under everyone. Each dines alone tonight. I kept to my own table; some nights I eat a friend's portion as well.",
      oathbroker: "Each of the undersigned is marked and individually liable. The fine print: no circle may overlap another's. Separate accounts. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A mark over every head. Each spoke alone. I gave each its own floor. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Marks on every head. Fanned out like a hunting party, each to our own ground. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A mark over everyone's head, mine included, which was unforgivably public. I slid off to my own patch, alone, as I prefer.",
      pyromancer: "A mark over every head! Each of us needed our own floor, so I claimed a grand patch all to myself. Nobody singed.",
      thermist: "Marks over every head. Apart, cold: each to our own floor. Then back in, hot.",
      driftblade: "Marks over all our heads and each needed its own floor, so I ran out through the gaps to the far side and circled there alone and came back through after.",
      marionettist: "Every member of the company marked, each needing a solo spot. The company fans out across the stage. (A rare scene with everyone in the spotlight.)",
      cleric: "A mark over everyone's head, each needing their own floor. I sent each of my friends to their own patch, and mended them all after.",
      grovekeeper: "A mark over every head, like seedlings needing their own soil. My lot planted themselves apart, and I watered them all after.",
      chronomancer: "Marks over every head; on the next beat each lands. Each of us apart by then. I will have been alone already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a mark on everyone. Remedy: everyone apart, no circles touching, and a dose for all after. One small fire, deliberate.",
      threadweaver: "A mark over each head. I let the threads go slack so each stitch had its own floor, then pulled them all tight again after.",
    } },
    'The King Rises': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He rose from his throne, and his knights took new posts on the walls. The line had to learn new ground. I took him back and turned him away from them. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one stood up again! And his knights moved, so everything from the walls came from new places. Back to his face, at last.",
      runeguard: "The omen: he rises, and his knights move to new posts. The answer: read the hall again; every echo now comes from where they stand now, not where they stood. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "My host returned to the table, and his guard changed seats. The leftovers come from new places now. I took my seat across from him again.",
      oathbroker: "He resumed, his knights in new positions. The fine print: their testimony now comes from the new ones. The undersigned noted the change. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "He stood. His knights moved. Their words come from new places now. I went back to him.",
      ranger: "It rose. Knights moved to new walls. New angles. Read them again.",
      shadowblade: "The mark rose, and its knights took new places by the walls. Its back is still where it always was. I went straight back there.",
      pyromancer: "He rose from his throne and his knights moved to new places, so everything they copy comes from somewhere new! I moved my spot, grumbling.",
      thermist: "He rose. Knights moved. New walls, new angles. Learned them cold. Then back in, hot.",
      driftblade: "He rose and the knights shifted to new walls, so the lanes I'd worked out were wrong, and I ran a fresh circle through the hall to learn the new ones.",
      marionettist: "The star returns from intermission! The understudies take new marks on the walls, and the company re-blocks everything. (Entr'acte concluded.)",
      cleric: "He rose from his throne and his old knights took new places. I gathered my friends and showed them where the echoes would come from now.",
      grovekeeper: "He rose like a frost lifting, and his knights moved to new ground. Their weather comes from new fields now. My lot adjusted.",
      chronomancer: "He rises; the knights move. The echoes come from new places now. I'll have adjusted before it happened.",
      alembist: "Note: he rises, and his knights move to new posts. Relabel the shelves; the echoes come from new places now.",
      threadweaver: "He rose, and his knights moved to new walls. I pinned my little stitches to the new pattern.",
    } },
    'Royal Tithe': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He took his tithe, and every one of us dropped low at once. Shields on us beforehand stayed. He held his blows off me while the healers mended the line before the Dirge. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one took his tithe: every one of us dropped near death at once. Glorious! For me. The others needed shields before it and mending after, before the Dirge.",
      runeguard: "The omen: he raises a hand over all of us. The answer: shields laid before, for they are not life and he cannot take them; then every one of us mended before the Dirge. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host demanded a tithe: everyone drained low at once. Shields aren't life; he can't take them. The Dirge follows quickly, so mend before it. A meal that leaves you hungrier.",
      oathbroker: "The Tithe levies life from every one of the undersigned at once. The fine print: shields are not life and stay; the Dirge comes soon after. Mend everyone before it falls due.",
      ironpalm: "He took his tithe of all of us. The shields stayed. Then quiet. Mend, before the Dirge. I breathed.",
      ranger: "Tithe drops all of us low. Shields stay. Then the Dirge. Healers mend first. Kept drawing.",
      shadowblade: "The mark took a tithe of life from all of us at once. Very rude. Shields stay, and the healers mended everyone before the Dirge. It never saw me tremble.",
      pyromancer: "He took a tithe! Every one of us dropped low! I felt terribly pale. Our shields stayed, the healers mended us, and the Dirge washed over us harmlessly.",
      thermist: "Tithe. Everyone low. Cold. Shields stay. Then mending, fast, hot, before the Dirge.",
      driftblade: "He took his tithe and every one of us dropped low at once, so I stayed close to the healers for once, still running circles, and let them mend me before the Dirge.",
      marionettist: "The star takes his tithe from the whole company, and everyone drops low. Shields remain; the menders bring everyone back up before the Dirge. (A tense scene.)",
      cleric: "He took his tithe from all of us at once. Shields aren't life, so I laid them first. Then I mended everyone before the Dirge. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "The tithe takes from all my lot at once, like a drought. Plant shields before; they stay. Then mend everyone before the Dirge.",
      chronomancer: "The tithe lands; a moment later, the Dirge. Shields before the first beat, mending between. I'll have done it before, and did.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the Tithe, all of us drained low. Remedy: shields before, since he can't take those, then a big dose for everyone before the Dirge. Shelves emptied.",
      threadweaver: "He took his tithe from everyone at once. Shields before; they stay. Then I pulled the whole hem back up before the Dirge. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Dirge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Dirge tolled through all of us after the tithe. No blocking it. The line came to it whole, mended in time. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The Dirge comes right after the tithe. Small, but if you're still low it kills you. I was mended. Barely a scratch.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the Dirge follows the tithe a breath later. The answer: everyone mended before it tolls. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The Dirge is the after-dinner song, sung to all, and it kills those left hungry from the tithe. Mended before. Dull tune.",
      oathbroker: "The Dirge is the final instalment of the Tithe, due from all. The fine print: you must be mended before it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The Dirge spoke to all of us. Those still low could not have answered. We were mended. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Dirge after the tithe. Everyone, no cover. Be mended before. I was.",
      shadowblade: "The Dirge tolled for all of us, quietly. Anyone still low from the tithe would not have survived. I was mended. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The Dirge tolled through all of us! Only a soft song, but anyone still low from the tithe would have dropped. I was mended in time. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Dirge after the tithe. Soft, cold. Kills only the low. Mended in time. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The Dirge tolls right after the tithe, soft, but fatal to anyone still low, so I got myself mended before it, still circling.",
      marionettist: "The Dirge, sung to the whole company. Fatal only to those still low from the tithe. The menders brought everyone up in time. (A sombre number.)",
      cleric: "The Dirge tolled right after the tithe. Soft, but fatal to anyone still low. I had everyone mended before it. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The Dirge follows the tithe like rain after drought. Soft, but my lot must be green again before it comes. They were.",
      chronomancer: "The Dirge, a beat after the tithe. Soft, but it finishes the low. I'll have mended them all in time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the Dirge, soft, but fatal to the low. Remedy: everyone mended before it. Same shelf as the Tithe.",
      threadweaver: "The Dirge tolled through the whole hem after the tithe. Soft, but it snaps any thread still frayed. I had them all mended.",
    } },
    'Judgement': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Judgement: the swings from the banners, in that order, from him and both knights at once, each shown for only an instant. I called the order to the line from memory. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Judgement! All the banner swings, from him and both knights at once, in order, barely shown. I knew the order and stepped right. Not a scratch.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the charges returned in order, from him and his knights together, each barely drawn. The answer: the order inscribed earlier; move a beat before each shows, and close on him for the Crown. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Judgement: the menu returns, every course at once, from my host and both his knights, each barely glimpsed. I knew the order. Ate nothing; dodged everything.",
      oathbroker: "Judgement enforces the charges in their listed order, from him and his knights together, each shown only briefly. The fine print was in the Charge Sheet. The undersigned recalled it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Judgement. Everything he said on the banners, said again, all at once, from all of them. I knew the words before they came. I answered.",
      ranger: "Judgement. Banner order, from it and both knights at once, barely shown. Moved before each one landed. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Judgement: every swing on the banners, in order, from the mark and both knights at once, each shown only an instant. I knew the order. It never saw me move.",
      pyromancer: "Judgement! Every swing from the banners, from him and both knights, in order, barely shown! I remembered! I moved! Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Judgement. The banners' order, cold in memory. Then the swings, hot and fast, from him and both knights. Move ahead of each.",
      driftblade: "Judgement throws the banner swings back in order from him and both knights at once, barely shown, so I ran the route I'd learned from the banners, through each gap a beat early.",
      marionettist: "Judgement: the programme performed in full, in order, by the star and both understudies at once, every scene barely lit. The company knew the running order. (Standing ovation.)",
      cleric: "Judgement: every swing from the banners, in order, from him and both knights at once. I led my friends through it from memory, close to him for the ring. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "Judgement: all the banner seasons at once, in order, from him and both knights. My lot knew the order and moved before each.",
      chronomancer: "Judgement plays the banner melody back fast, from him and both knights. I knew the tune; we moved on the beat before each note.",
      alembist: "Ailment: Judgement, every banner swing in order, from him and both knights at once. Remedy: the order, memorised earlier. Out of each circle, out of each front, in close for the ring.",
      threadweaver: "Judgement: every banner swing in order, from him and both knights. My little stitches moved together, from memory. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'The Long Night': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The braziers died and one lantern was left. Doom passed from one of us to the next, the next one's sigil glowing ahead. Whoever was cured carried the light on. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The Long Night: one lantern left, and doom passing round us. The next one glows ahead; the lantern goes to them, and they carry it on. I carried it once. Grim and wonderful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the next to be doomed glows ahead of time. The answer: whoever holds the lantern walks it to them; once cured, they carry it on to the next. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host blew out every candle but one. Doom passes round the table, and the next guest glows ahead. Bring the lantern to them; they carry it on. A dessert to share.",
      oathbroker: "The Long Night: a single lantern, and Doom passed from one co-signatory to the next. The fine print: the next glows ahead. Bring the lantern; they inherit it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The dark came. One lantern. Doom passed among us, the next one glowing ahead. I brought the light. Then they carried it. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Last lantern. Doom moves round us. Next one glows ahead. Carry the light to them; they carry it on.",
      shadowblade: "The Long Night: every light out but one lantern. Doom passes round us, and the next one glows ahead. I carried the light to them in the dark. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The Long Night: every fire out but one lantern! Doom passes round us, and the next one glows ahead. I carried the light to them, the brightest thing in the hall!",
      thermist: "One lantern. Doom moves. Next one glows. Carry it to them. Cold. Then they take it on, and I run hot.",
      driftblade: "The Long Night: one lantern, doom passing from one of us to the next, so I carried it through the dark to whoever glowed next, and they took it on from there.",
      marionettist: "The finale: every light out but one lantern, passed hand to hand as doom moves through the company. The next one glows ahead. (A beautiful relay.)",
      cleric: "The Long Night: one lantern, and doom passing among us. The next one glows ahead; whoever holds the light brings it to them. Thank the light, quite literally.",
      grovekeeper: "The Long Night: one lantern, doom moving through my lot like a long winter. The next glows ahead; the light goes to them, and they carry it on.",
      chronomancer: "The Long Night: doom passes from one to the next, the next glowing a beat ahead. The lantern follows. I'll have carried it already.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the Long Night. One lantern, doom passing round. Remedy: carry the lantern to whoever glows next; once cured, they carry it on. One reagent, shared.",
      threadweaver: "The Long Night: one lantern, doom passing round my little stitches. The next one glows ahead; the light goes to them, and they carry it on. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Gravebind': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A grave-chain bound two of us, never me. They kept close until it broke, and the rest kept apart under their marks. I took the greatsword. Three duties at once, and the pair is drawn fresh every pull. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "A grave-chain bound two of the others, a different pair each pull! They stayed close; the rest went apart under their marks. I took the greatsword. A busy night.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a chain between two of the party, never the knight, a different pair each time. The answer: the bound pair stay close until it breaks; the others apart under their marks; and I take the greatsword.",
      gorgemaw: "A chain bound two of my guests together, and it was a different two than last time. They stayed close; the others dined apart. I had the greatsword. Several courses at once.",
      oathbroker: "Gravebind joins two of the undersigned, never the shield, a different pair each hearing. The fine print: they must stay close until it breaks; the others must keep apart. I took the greatsword. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A chain bound two of us. A different two each time. They stayed close. The others kept apart. I took the greatsword. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Chain on two of us, not the tank, different each time. The pair stayed close till it broke. The others kept apart, under their marks.",
      shadowblade: "A grave-chain bound me to another, a different one each night. We kept close until it broke; the rest kept apart. Very public. It never saw me, at least.",
      pyromancer: "A chain bound me to somebody! We stayed close, terribly close, until it broke. It's a different pair each time. The others kept apart under their marks. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Chained to another, not the same one as last time. Stay close. Cold rule. Others apart, under marks. Then it broke, and I ran hot.",
      driftblade: "A grave-chain bound me to somebody, so I couldn't run, so I circled them close until it broke, while the others kept apart under their marks. Never the same pair twice.",
      marionettist: "A grave-chain binds two of the company together, never the lead, and they stay close until it breaks. The pairing changes every night. The rest keep apart. (My Doll and I were not bound. A relief.)",
      cleric: "A grave-chain bound me to one of the others, a different pair on every night. We kept close until it broke. The others kept apart under their marks. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "A chain bound two of my lot together, like vines grown into each other. A different two each season. We stayed close until it broke. The rest kept apart.",
      chronomancer: "A chain bound two of us, never the knight, a different two each time. We stayed close until it broke, a few beats later. The others apart. I'll have known.",
      alembist: "Ailment: Gravebind. Two of us, chained, drawn fresh each pull. Remedy: stay close until it breaks. The rest apart under marks. Note: the greatsword falls on our knight at the same time.",
      threadweaver: "A grave-chain bound two of my little stitches, a different two every time. They kept close, no slack, until it broke. The rest kept apart. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Burial Rites': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He took the middle of the hall and dug a grave for each of us, each in our own colour. Which grave is whose changes every pull; read your colour. Lines out, in close for the crown, then into my own grave before it shut. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "The big one dug us graves! One each, our own colour, but the places shuffle every pull. In close for his crown, then out and lie in your own. The one who strayed got a lovely wound, and we all paid for it.",
      runeguard: "The omen: four graves round him, each in its owner's colour, their places drawn anew each pull. The answer: the writ, the crown in close, then each to their own before it closes; never another's. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host set four graves, one per guest, each in their own colour, and shuffled the seats each time. After his crown we each took our own place. Sit in another's and you're served a beating. Well presented.",
      oathbroker: "Each of the undersigned is allotted a grave in their own colour, the allotment drawn anew each hearing. Terms: be in your own when it closes. The fine print: lying in another's is billed as well, and an empty grave raises the dead on us all.",
      ironpalm: "He gave each of us a grave, in our own colour. The places change each time. In close for his crown. Then out, each to our own. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Four graves, each our colour. They move around between pulls, so look at the colours. Crown in close, then out, plenty of time. Never step in another's.",
      shadowblade: "The mark dug us each a grave in our own colour, in a new arrangement every time. After the crown I slipped out to mine, quietly. Lie in someone else's and it's rather rude of you, and it hurts. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He dug me a grave! In my own colour, which was thoughtful, though they shuffle between pulls. In close for his crown, then out to my own before it closed. Someone else's would have hurt. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: four graves, our colours, shuffled each pull. Crown first, in close. Then out to mine. Hot: someone stood in the wrong one and the dead rose for us all. Back to the middle after.",
      driftblade: "He dug four graves in our colours, in a new order every pull, and I circled him through the writ and the crown and then ran out to mine and lay still in it, which I hate, but only for a breath.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man lays out four graves, each lit in its player's colour, the blocking changed every night. The company comes in close for his crown, then exits each to their own grave. (The Doll has no grave. A relief.)",
      cleric: "He opened a grave for each of us, in our own colours, and which is whose is drawn fresh each time, so I read the colours. After his crown we each went to our own. Our young archer lay in mine once, and was struck for it. Dawn keep her.",
      grovekeeper: "Four graves, dug in our own colours, in different beds each season. My lot came in close for his crown, then each went out to their own and lay still. An empty bed, and the dead come up for us all.",
      chronomancer: "The graves open in our colours, the places drawn anew each pull, and close a while later, after the writ and the crown. I'll have walked out to mine in good time. I did; plenty of beats to spare.",
      alembist: "Ailment: four graves, one per hero, each labelled in their colour, the labels reshuffled each pull. Remedy: in close for the crown, then everyone to their own before it shuts. Note: an empty grave raises the dead on us all.",
      threadweaver: "He dug a grave for each of my little stitches, each in its own colour, the places drawn fresh each pull. After the crown each went to their own. One wandered into the wrong one. A sloppy seam.",
    } },
    'Writ of Execution': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He chained every one of us and read the writ: a line of grave-light out through each, and it burns after. I pointed mine at a corner, between the graves, and stepped aside. One of us had a writ torn up, a different one each pull. Held.",
      bloodreaver: "Chains on all of us, then a line out through each! Take your own, keep it off the others. They stay burning, so aim between the graves. One of us was pardoned, a different one each pull, lucky thing, and missed a good scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a chain to each of us, one hanging broken. The answer: each points their line out between the graves, apart from the rest, and steps off; the lines burn on until the graves close. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My host chained his guests and sent a course of grave-light down each chain. Aim yours toward a corner, between the graves, for the line stays hot on the table. One guest's writ was torn up: no serving. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "The writ names each of the undersigned, save one, whose is torn in two. Terms: point yours between the graves and away from the others, then step aside. The fine print: the line keeps burning, and one aimed at a grave closes the way. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "He chained us all and read the writ. A line out through each of us. I sent mine between the graves, then stepped away. It kept burning. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Chain on each of us; a line out through each. Mine to the corner, between graves, apart from the rest, then step off. It burns after. Aimed at a grave, it walls you out. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark chained us all and read out a line for each. I sent mine into a corner between the graves and stepped out of it. The lines stay burning, so a careless aim bars someone's way home. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He read a writ and a line of grave-fire ran out through each of us! I aimed mine at a corner, between the graves, and hopped aside. It kept burning, which I admired. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Cold: chains, a line each. Point it between the graves. Step off. Hot: it kept burning on the floor, a spoke out of him, and I nearly ran through it to my grave. Too far.",
      driftblade: "Every one of us chained and a line out through each, so I ran mine out to a corner between the graves and stepped off it, and later threaded through the burning spokes and out to my grave.",
      marionettist: "Our leading man reads a writ: a line from him through each player. The company points theirs to the corners, between the graves, and steps aside; the lines burn on as scenery. (One writ is torn up. Not mine.)",
      cleric: "He chained us all, but my writ was torn, so I was free to stand where I could reach everyone. The others aimed their lines between the graves and stepped aside. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "My writ was torn, so I stood clear of the others' lines and kept mending. They each sent their line toward a corner, between the graves, like rows planted so the paths stay open. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Pardoned, this time, which was last time: my writ was torn. The others aimed theirs between the graves and stepped off as it fired. The lines burn on until the graves close. I'll have known.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the Writ, a line through each of us that stays burning. Remedy: aim it between the graves, away from the others, step off. Note: my own writ came torn, so I brewed where I liked.",
      threadweaver: "My writ was torn, so I tended my little stitches from wherever I liked. Each sent their line out to a corner, between the graves, so the burning lines left every path open. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
  },
};
